== God Justifies The Ungodly

#smallcaps[Listen] to a little sermon. You will find the text in the Epistle to the Romans, in the fourth chapter and the fifth verse:

#quote[“To the one who does not work, but believes in Him who justifies the ungodly, his faith is credited as righteousness.”]

I want to draw your attention to those words, “Him who justifies the ungodly.” They seem to me very wonderful words.

Are you not surprised that there should be such an expression as that in the Bible, “Who justifies the ungodly”? I have heard that men who hate the doctrines of the cross bring it as a charge against God that he saves wicked men and receives to himself the vilest of the vile. See how this Scripture accepts the charge and plainly states it! By the mouth of his servant Paul, by the inspiration of the Holy Spirit, he takes to himself the title of “Him who justifies the ungodly.” He makes those just who are unjust, forgives those who deserve to be punished, and favors those who deserve no favor. You thought, did you not, that salvation was for the good? That God’s grace was for the pure and holy, who are free from sin? It has come into your head that if you were excellent, then God would reward you; and you have thought that because you are not worthy, there could be no way for you to enjoy his favor. You must be somewhat surprised to read a text like this: “Him who justifies the ungodly.” I do not wonder that you are surprised; for with all my familiarity with the great grace of God, I never stop wondering at it. It does sound surprising, does it not, that it should be possible for a holy God to justify an unholy man? We, following the natural legalism of our hearts, are always talking about our own goodness and our own worthiness, and we stubbornly insist that there must be something in us to win God’s notice. Now God, who sees through all deceptions, knows that there is no goodness whatever in us. He says that “there is no one righteous, not even one.” He knows that “all our righteous acts are like filthy rags”; and therefore the Lord Jesus did not come into the world to look for goodness and righteousness among men, but to bring goodness and righteousness with him and give them to people who have none of them. He comes, not because we #emph[are] just, but to make us so: he justifies the ungodly.

When a counselor comes into court, if he is an honest man, he wants to plead the case of an innocent person and justify him before the court when charges are falsely laid against him. It should be the lawyer’s aim to justify the innocent person, and he should not try to shield the guilty party. It does not lie in man’s right nor in man’s power truly to justify the guilty. This is a miracle reserved for the Lord alone. God, the infinitely just Sovereign, knows that there is not a just man on earth who does good and does not sin, and therefore, in the infinite sovereignty of his divine nature and in the splendor of his inexpressible love, he undertakes the task, not so much of justifying the just as of justifying the ungodly. God has devised ways and means of making the ungodly man stand justly accepted before him: he has set up a system by which, with perfect justice, he can treat the guilty man as if he had been free from offense all his life, yes, can treat him as if he were wholly free from sin. He justifies the ungodly.

Jesus Christ came into the world to save #emph[sinners]. It is a very surprising thing — a thing to be marveled at most of all by those who enjoy it. I know that to me, even to this day, it is the greatest wonder I ever heard of, that God should ever justify #emph[me]. I feel myself to be a lump of unworthiness, a mass of corruption, and a heap of sin, apart from his almighty love. I know with full assurance that I am justified by faith which is in Christ Jesus, and treated as if I had been perfectly just, and made an heir of God and a joint-heir with Christ; and yet by nature I must take my place among the most sinful. I, who am altogether undeserving, am treated as if I had been deserving. I am loved with as much love as if I had always been godly, whereas before I was ungodly. Who can help being astonished at this? Gratitude for such favor stands dressed in robes of wonder.

Now, while this is very surprising, I want you to notice how available it makes the gospel to you and to me. If God justifies the #emph[ungodly], then, dear friend, he can justify #emph[you]. Is that not the very kind of person you are? If you are unconverted at this moment, it describes you exactly: you have lived without God, you have been the reverse of godly; in one word, you have been and are #emph[ungodly]. Perhaps you have not even gone to a place of worship on the Sabbath, but have lived in disregard of God’s day, and house, and Word — this proves you to have been ungodly. Sadder still, it may be that you have even tried to doubt God’s existence, and have gone so far as to say that you did. You have lived on this fair earth, which is full of the signs of God’s presence, and all the while you have shut your eyes to the clear evidence of his power and Godhead. You have lived as if there were no God. Indeed, you would have been very pleased if you could have proved to yourself for certain that there was no God at all. Possibly you have lived a great many years in this way, so that you are now pretty well settled in your ways, and yet God is not in any of them. If you were labeled #smallcaps[ungodly], it would describe you as well as the label #emph[salt water] would describe the sea. Would it not?

Or perhaps you are a person of another sort; you have regularly kept all the outward forms of religion, and yet you have had no heart in them at all, but have really been ungodly. Though meeting with the people of God, you have never met with God for yourself; you have been in the choir, and yet have not praised the Lord with your heart. You have lived without any love for God in your heart, or regard for his commands in your life. Well, you are just the kind of man to whom this gospel is sent — this gospel which says that God justifies #emph[the ungodly]. It is very wonderful, but it is happily available for you. It just suits you. Does it not? How I wish that you would accept it! If you are a sensible man, you will see the remarkable grace of God in providing for people like you, and you will say to yourself, “Justify the ungodly! Why, then, should I not be justified, and justified at once?”

Now, notice further that #emph[it must be so] — that the salvation of God is for those who do not deserve it and have no preparation for it. It is reasonable that the statement should be put in the Bible; for, dear friend, no one needs justifying except those who have no justification of their own. If any of my readers are perfectly righteous, they need no justifying. You feel that you are doing your duty well, and almost putting heaven in your debt. What do you want with a Savior, or with mercy? What do you want with justification? You will be tired of my book by this time, for it will hold no interest for you.

If any of you#super[+] are giving yourselves#super[+] such proud airs, listen to me for a little while. You#super[+] will be lost, as sure as you#super[+] are alive. You#super[+] righteous men, whose righteousness is all of your#super[+] own working, are either deceivers or deceived; for the Scripture cannot lie, and it says plainly, “There is no one righteous, not even one.” In any case I have no gospel to preach to the self-righteous — no, not a word of it. Jesus Christ himself did not come to call the righteous, and I am not going to do what he did not do. If I called you#super[+], you#super[+] would not come, and so I will not call you#super[+] as righteous. No, I urge you#super[+] instead to look at that righteousness of yours#super[+] till you#super[+] see what a delusion it is. It is not half as substantial as a cobweb. Be done with it! Flee from it! O friends, the only people who can need justification are those who are not just in themselves. They need something to be done for them to make them just before the judgment seat of God. Depend on it, the Lord does only what is needful. Infinite wisdom never attempts what is unnecessary. Jesus never undertakes what is superfluous. To make him just who #emph[is] just is no work for God — that would be a labor for a fool; but to make him just who is unjust — that is work for infinite love and mercy. To justify the ungodly — this is a miracle worthy of a God. And it certainly is so.

Now, look. If there is anywhere in the world a physician who has discovered sure and precious remedies, to whom is that physician sent? To those who are perfectly healthy? I think not. Put him down in a district where there are no sick people, and he feels that he is out of place. There is nothing for him to do. “It is not the healthy who need a physician, but the sick.” Is it not equally clear that the great remedies of grace and redemption are for the sick in soul? They cannot be for the healthy, for they can be of no use to them. If you, dear friend, feel that you are spiritually sick, the Physician has come into the world for you. If you are altogether ruined by your sin, you are the very person the plan of salvation aims at. I say that the Lord of love had just such people as you in his eye when he arranged the system of grace. Suppose a man of generous spirit were to resolve to forgive all those who were in debt to him; clearly this can only apply to those really in his debt. One person owes him a thousand dollars; another owes him fifty dollars; each one has only to have his bill marked paid, and the debt is wiped out. But the most generous person cannot forgive the debts of those who do not owe him anything. It is out of the power of omnipotence to forgive where there is no sin. Pardon, therefore, cannot be for you who have no sin. Pardon must be for the guilty. Forgiveness must be for the sinful. It would be absurd to talk of forgiving those who do not need forgiveness — pardoning those who have never offended.

Do you think that you must be lost because you are a sinner? That is the very reason why you can be saved. Because you acknowledge yourself to be a sinner, I would encourage you to believe that grace is ordained for people like you. One of our hymn writers even dared to say—

#quote[“A sinner is a sacred thing; \ The Holy Ghost hath made him so.”]

It is really true that Jesus seeks and saves what is lost. He died and made a real atonement for real sinners. When men are not playing with words, or calling themselves “miserable sinners” out of mere compliment, I am overjoyed to meet them. I would be glad to talk all night to #emph[bona fide] sinners. The inn of mercy never closes its doors on such people, on weekdays or on Sundays. Our Lord Jesus did not die for imaginary sins, but his heart’s blood was spilled to wash out deep crimson stains, which nothing else can remove.

The sinner black with sin — he is the kind of man that Jesus Christ came to make white. A gospel preacher once preached a sermon on “The ax lies ready at the root of the trees”; and he preached such a sermon that one of his hearers said to him, “One would have thought you had been preaching to criminals. Your sermon ought to have been given in the county jail.” “Oh, no,” said the good man, “if I were preaching in the county jail, I would not preach from that text. There I would preach, ‘This is a faithful saying, and worthy of full acceptance, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners.’” Just so. The law is for the self-righteous, to humble their pride: the gospel is for the lost, to remove their despair.

If you are not lost, what do you want with a Savior? Should the shepherd go after those who never went astray? Why should the woman sweep her house for coins that were never out of her purse? No, the medicine is for the diseased; the giving of life is for the dead; the pardon is for the guilty; liberation is for those who are bound; the opening of eyes is for those who are blind. How can the Savior, and his death on the cross, and the gospel of pardon, be accounted for, unless we suppose that men are guilty and deserve condemnation? The sinner is the gospel’s reason for existing. You, my friend, to whom this word now comes, if you are undeserving, ill-deserving, hell-deserving, you are the sort of man for whom the gospel is ordained, and arranged, and proclaimed. God justifies the ungodly.

I would like to make this very plain. I hope that I have done so already; but still, plain as it is, only the Lord can make a man see it. At first it does seem most amazing to an awakened man that salvation should really be for him as a lost and guilty one. He thinks that it must be for him as a penitent man, forgetting that his penitence is a part of his salvation. “Oh,” he says, “but I must be this and that” — all of which is true, for he will be this and that as the result of salvation; but salvation comes to him before he has any of the results of salvation. It comes to him, in fact, while he deserves only this bare, beggarly, base, abominable description, “#emph[ungodly].” That is all he is when God’s gospel comes to justify him.

May I, therefore, urge any who have no good thing about them — who fear that they have not even a good feeling, or anything at all that can recommend them to God — to believe firmly that our gracious God is able and willing to take them without anything to recommend them, and to forgive them spontaneously, not because #emph[they] are good, but because #emph[he] is good. Does he not make his sun shine on the evil as well as on the good? Does he not give fruitful seasons, and send the rain and the sunshine in their time on the most ungodly nations? Yes, even Sodom had its sun, and Gomorrah had its dew. O friend, the great grace of God goes beyond anything I can conceive and anything you can conceive, and I want you to think of it as it deserves. As high as the heavens are above the earth, so high are God’s thoughts above our thoughts. He can abundantly pardon. Jesus Christ came into the world to save sinners: forgiveness is for the guilty.

Do not try to touch yourself up and make yourself something other than you really are; but come as you are to him who justifies the ungodly. A great artist some time ago had painted some members of the council of the city in which he lived, and he wanted, for the sake of history, to include in his picture certain characters well known in the town. A street sweeper, unkempt, ragged, filthy, was known to everybody, and there was a suitable place for him in the picture. The artist said to this ragged and rugged fellow, “I will pay you well if you will come down to my studio and let me paint your portrait.” He came by in the morning, but he was soon sent on his way; for he had washed his face, and combed his hair, and put on a respectable suit of clothes. He was needed as a beggar, and was not invited in any other role. Even so, the gospel will receive you into its halls if you come as a sinner, but not otherwise. Do not wait to reform yourself, but come at once for salvation. God justifies #emph[the ungodly], and that picks #emph[you] up where you now are: it meets you in your worst state.

Come in your #emph[déshabille]. I mean, come to your heavenly Father in all your sin and sinfulness. Come to Jesus just as you are, leprous, filthy, naked, neither fit to live nor fit to die. Come, you who are the very sweepings of creation; come, though you hardly dare to hope for anything but death. Come, though despair is brooding over you, pressing on your chest like a horrible nightmare. Come and ask the Lord to justify another ungodly one. Why should he not? Come along, then; for this great mercy of God is meant for people like you. I put it in the words of the text, and I cannot put it more strongly: the Lord God himself takes to himself this gracious title, “Him who justifies the ungodly.” He makes just, and causes to be treated as just, those who by nature are ungodly. Is that not a wonderful word #emph[for you]? Reader, do not get up from your seat till you have thought this matter through.
